{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Then Israel sang this song.</b> Since it is not written 'Then Moses and the children of Israel sang,' and furthermore since they did not sing this song at the beginning of the forty years, hear from this that the children of Israel sang this song about Moses, that the Well returned in his merit after it had ceased with the death of Miriam. And for this reason Miriam is not mentioned in this song, while Moses is mentioned, as it says: ״בְּאֵר חֲפָרוּהָ שָׂרִים כָּרוּהָ נְדִיבֵי הָעָם בִּמְחֹקֵק״, and 'מְחֹקֵק' is none other than Moses, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:21): ״כִּי שָׁם חֶלְקַת מְחֹקֵק סָפוּן״. And in Ta'anit (9a) it concludes that it returned in the merit of Moses — for at first the manna was in the merit of Moses, who received the Torah, for the Torah was given only to those who ate the manna (Tanhuma Beshalach 20), as it says (Exodus 16:4): ״לְמַעַן אֲנַסֶּנּוּ הֲיֵלֵךְ בְּתוֹרָתִי״; and the Well, which supplied all their needs, was in the merit of Miriam, who held fast to the trait of lovingkindness, as it says (Exodus 1:18): ״וַתְּחַיֶּיןָ אֶת הַיְלָדִים״ — they would provide them food, etc.; and the clouds of glory, in which rested the glory of the Shekhinah, were in the merit of Aaron, who caused the indwelling of the Shekhinah through the offerings; and in the merit of the Torah all three of them returned. Therefore it says, 'a well which the nobles of the people dug' — who held fast to the trait of lovingkindness, and in their merit the Well existed from of old and from earliest times; now it returned 'through the lawgiver' (בִּמְחֹקֵק), in the merit of Moses and in the merit of the Torah, which is alluded to in the verse ״וּמִמִּדְבָּר מַתָּנָה וּמִמַּתָּנָה נַחֲלִיאֵל״, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Eruvin (54b). For if not so, it is difficult: what did they see to interpret these verses as referring to the Torah, and what does the praise of the Torah have to do with the song of the Well? But it is because the Well returned in the merit of the Torah that Moses received. And this interpretation is correct and clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The citation of Exodus 1:18 (וַתְּחַיֶּיןָ אֶת הַיְלָדִים) more precisely corresponds to Exodus 1:17 in standard verse numbering; the Kli Yakar follows the rabbinic citation convention (e.g., Talmud Ta'anit 9a) which numbers this verse as 1:18."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:23.942037157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}